3 |\n| 7 | 21 | 6 | 9 |\n| 8 | 16 | 2 | 6 |\n| 9 | 9 | 5 | 6 |\n\n$$f(11)\\equiv 0+2+6+1+1+11+3+9+6+6 = 45 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{13}$$\n\n**$f(12)$:** $140\\equiv 8\\pmod{12}$, so exponents cycle $\\{9,3,1\\}$ (i.e.\\ $2^8,2^4,2^0$).\n\n| $k$ | $(11{-}k)k$ | power mod 13 | product mod 13 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 0 | 9 | 0 |\n| 1 | 10 | 3 | 4 |\n| 2 | 18 | 1 | 5 |\n| 3 | 24 | 9 | 8 |\n| 4 | 28 | 3 | 6 |\n| 5 | 30 | 1 | 4 |\n| 6 | 30 | 9 | 10 |\n| 7 | 28 | 3 | 6 |\n| 8 | 24 | 1 | 11 |\n| 9 | 18 | 9 | 6 |\n| 10 | 10 | 3 | 4 |\n\n$$f(12)\\equiv 0+4+5+8+6+4+10+6+11+6+4 = 64 \\equiv 12 \\pmod{13}$$\n\n**Final answer:**\n\n$$f(10)+f(11)+f(12)\\equiv 10+6+12 = 28 \\equiv \\boxed{2}\\pmod{13}$$\n\n### PROOF COMPLETE", "thinking": "Let me solve this problem. I need to find f(10) + f(11) + f(12) mod 13.\n\nThe recurrence is:\nf(0) = f(1) = 0\nf(n+2) = 4^{n+2} f(n+1) - 16^{n+1} f(n) + n · 2^{n^2}\n\nLet me think about this. The coefficients 4^{n+2} and 16^{n+1} = (4^2)^{n+1} = 4^{2n+2}. And the forcing term n·2^{n^2}.\n\nLet me try a substitution. Notice 4 = 2^2, 16 = 2^4. Let me try f(n) = 2^{n^2} g(n) or something similar.\n\nActually, let me think about the homogeneous part:\nf(n+2) = 4^{n+2} f(n+1) - 16^{n+1} f(n)\n\nLet me try f(n) = c^n · h(n) or f(n) = 2^{an^2 + bn} · g(n).\n\nActually, let me try f(n) = 2^{n^2} g(n). Then:\n2^{(n+2)^2} g(n+2) = 4^{n+2} · 2^{(n+1)^2} g(n+1) - 16^{n+1} · 2^{n^2} g(n) + n·2^{n^2}\n\nLeft: 2^{n^2+4n+4} g(n+2)\nFirst term: 2^{2n+4} · 2^{n^2+2n+1} g(n+1) = 2^{n^2+4n+5} g(n+1)\nSecond term: 2^{4n+4} · 2^{n^2} g(n) = 2^{n^2+4n+4} g(n)\nForcing: n·2^{n^2}\n\nDivide everything by 2^{n^2+4n+4}:\ng(n+2) = 2 g(n+1) - g(n) + n / 2^{4n+4}\n\nHmm, that gives g(n+2) = 2g(n+1) - g(n) + n·2^{-4n-4}.\n\nThe homogeneous part g(n+2) - 2g(n+1) + g(n) = 0 has solution g(n) = A + Bn.\n\nThe particular solution for n·2^{-4n-4} is messy. Let me try a different substitution.\n\nActually, maybe try f(n) = 2^{n^2 - something}. Let me reconsider.\n\nLet me try f(n) = 2